        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: It Changes the Habitat, It Is Not an Insecticide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Silverfish are moisture specialists. University extension services consistently describe them as insects of damp places: Texas A&amp;M AgriLife gives their preferred conditions as 70 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit with 75 to 95 percent relative humidity, and the University of California IPM program notes that at warmer temperatures they need humidity above 75 percent. Most homes, kept dry, never get near that. The places that do are basements, bathrooms, laundry rooms, the cabinet under the kitchen sink, and any spot near a leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is exactly the territory a dehumidifier controls. Bring a basement down from the 65 to 80 percent it might sit at in summer to a steady 45 to 50 percent, and the space stops meeting silverfish needs. Eggs and young are the most vulnerable stages, and populations in persistently dry conditions dwindle over time. UC IPM lists using dehumidifiers in closed spaces as one of its core non-chemical controls, and North Carolina State Extension recommends drying out damp areas with a fan or dehumidifier alongside fixing leaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it does not do is kill the insects already there in any quick or direct way. Silverfish shelter in wall voids, behind baseboards, inside stacked cardboard and under insulation, where the air can stay damper than the room average for a long time. UC IPM notes they can go several months without food, and NC State puts adult lifespan at roughly two to eight years. A population that has been living comfortably in a damp basement for a while has staying power.
          </p>

          <p>
            So think of a dehumidifier as the thing that stops the problem coming back rather than the thing that ends it. Every successful plan in the extension literature combines the same four elements: reduce humidity, remove food and clutter, seal hiding places, and use a dust, bait or trap to reduce the existing population. Dehumidifying is the one that keeps working after the other three are done, which is why it belongs at the centre rather than as an afterthought.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Necessary, not sufficient.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Dry air is what keeps silverfish from coming back. Getting rid of the ones already in your home takes food removal, sealing and a dust, bait or trap on top.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Silverfish Depend on Humid Air
          </h2>

          <p>
            Silverfish are among the most primitive insects alive: wingless, roughly half an inch long as adults, carrot-shaped, covered in silvery scales and trailing three long bristles from the tail end. They are nocturnal and fast, and they shun light, which is why most people see them only when they turn on a bathroom light at night or lift a box that has sat on a basement floor for a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their dependence on humidity comes down to water. Small insects with a large surface area relative to their body lose water quickly, and silverfish are adapted to environments where that loss is easy to replace from the air around them and from damp surfaces. In dry air they lose water faster than they can take it in, which is why they cluster in the dampest parts of a home and why they turn up in bathtubs and sinks, where they have often fallen in looking for moisture and cannot climb the smooth sides back out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity also governs how fast they breed. NC State Extension notes that a female may lay more than 100 eggs over her lifetime, which hatch in three to six weeks. UC IPM adds that development is slow in cool conditions: below about 50 degrees, silverfish can take up to 500 days to mature, while in warm conditions they can mature in about 60. A warm, humid basement in summer is close to ideal for them; a cool, dry one is close to the opposite.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why a silverfish problem is so often a moisture problem in disguise. If you are finding them regularly, ask where the water is. A bathroom without a working exhaust fan, a slow drip under a sink, condensation on cold water pipes, a damp basement wall or a roof leak into an attic are the usual suspects. Fixing the source does more than a dehumidifier can, and our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> walks through finding where it is coming from.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same moisture that attracts silverfish attracts other pests too, notably the house centipedes that hunt them. If you are seeing both, the two problems share a root cause, and our guide to <a href="/does-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-centipedes/">whether a dehumidifier gets rid of centipedes</a> covers the predator side of the same equation. Dry the space and both lose their reason to stay.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="target" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Humidity to Target, and How Long It Takes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Set the dehumidifier to 45 to 50 percent and leave it there. That is well below the 75 percent extension sources give as the silverfish comfort zone, it is in the range generally recommended for keeping mould and dust mites down, and it does not make the machine work so hard that running costs climb steeply. There is no benefit to driving a basement down to 35 percent for silverfish; the gain over 50 is small and the electricity bill is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure where the insects actually live, not where the dehumidifier sits. A separate digital hygrometer costs very little and is the only way to know what the air is doing. Put one on a shelf among stored boxes, another near the floor against an outside wall, and check them after a week. The room average can read 50 percent while the air trapped inside a cardboard box pressed against a cold foundation wall is still far wetter. Those pockets are where silverfish hold out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air movement is what closes those pockets. Pull stored items a few inches away from exterior walls, get boxes off the floor onto shelving, and keep a path for air to circulate around the space. If the basement is divided into rooms, either keep the doors open or accept that a single unit will dry one room well and the others less so. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/">where to place a dehumidifier in a basement</a> covers the positions that reach the most of the space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set expectations on timing. Because adults can survive months without food and sheltered spots dry slowly, a dehumidifier alone typically shows its effect over a season rather than a week. With food removal, sealing and a dust or bait added, most households see sightings fall noticeably within a few weeks and continue falling over the following two to three months as eggs that were already laid hatch into conditions they cannot thrive in.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not already own a dehumidifier, the capacity you need is set by the size and dampness of the space, not by the insects. A damp 1,000-square-foot basement typically needs a 50-pint unit under current ratings, and a bucket-only model that stops when full is no use against a pest that needs consistent dryness. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">guide to choosing a dehumidifier</a> covers capacity, drainage and the cold-basement question in order.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Relative humidity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means for silverfish</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">75 to 95%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Preferred range per Texas A&amp;M AgriLife; breeding and development are fastest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60 to 75%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Still workable, especially in sheltered pockets and warm spaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">45 to 50%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recommended basement target; hostile to silverfish over time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Below 40%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No meaningful extra benefit against silverfish; higher running cost</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="firebrats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Silverfish or Firebrats? The One Case Dry Air Will Not Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you rely on a dehumidifier, make sure what you have is actually silverfish. Firebrats are close relatives with the same carrot shape and three tail bristles, and many people never tell them apart. The differences that matter are colour and where you find them. Silverfish are a fairly uniform silver or grey with a metallic sheen. Firebrats are mottled, with patches of brown and grey, and they turn up in the hottest parts of a home.
          </p>

          <p>
            That location is the key. UC IPM describes firebrats as thriving at around 98 to 102 degrees Fahrenheit and persisting at relative humidity as low as 30 percent. In a house, that means next to a furnace, boiler, water heater or clothes dryer, around hot water pipes, and in attics heated by the sun. NC State Extension describes them as favouring high temperatures of 90 degrees and above. They are, in other words, the one member of this family a dehumidifier does very little to discourage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Extension sources are not entirely consistent on how much firebrats care about humidity, but they all agree that heat is their main draw, and UC IPM specifically notes that boric acid dust used on its own is ineffective against them. If the insects you are finding cluster around the furnace or water heater rather than in the dampest corners, focus on sealing gaps around those appliances, removing stored paper and lint near them, and using a labelled insecticide dust or bait rather than expecting the dehumidifier to help.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most homes with a basement problem have silverfish, not firebrats, and the two are treated the same way apart from the humidity question. If you are not sure which you have, catch one on a sticky trap and compare it under good light; mottled and near heat means firebrat, uniform silver in a damp corner means silverfish. If you are still unsure, your local cooperative extension office will usually identify a specimen, often for free.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="food" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Take Away the Food: Paper, Starch and Cardboard
          </h2>

          <p>
            Silverfish eat starch and sugar in almost any form, which in a home means paper and the things that hold paper together. NC State Extension lists paper and fabric, especially glazed paper and book bindings containing starch or glue, along with dry carbohydrate foods and some high-protein foods such as dry pet food. UC IPM adds cereal, flour, pasta, starched clothing, household dust and dead insects. A basement full of cardboard boxes, old magazines and stored books is a pantry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cardboard is the worst offender because it is food and shelter in one. The corrugated layers hold moisture, the glue is edible, and a box sitting on a concrete floor against an outside wall is about the dampest, darkest, most undisturbed spot in the house. Replacing cardboard storage with lidded plastic bins does more for a silverfish problem than almost anything else you can do in an afternoon, and it protects whatever is inside from both insects and humidity at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work through the space methodically. Recycle old newspapers, magazines and empty boxes. Move books, photographs and papers you want to keep into sealed bins, and get those bins up on shelving off the floor. Store dry foods and pet food in airtight containers. Vacuum along baseboards, under shelving and in corners to remove dust, shed scales and dead insects, and empty the vacuum outside. UC IPM also suggests occasionally moving stored books and papers, since silverfish favour anything left undisturbed for long periods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know the damage signs so you can find the hot spots. Silverfish feeding leaves irregular holes or notches in paper, scraped patches on glossy surfaces such as photographs and magazine covers, ragged edges on book bindings, and sometimes yellowish stains. NC State Extension also lists cast skins, scales and small pepper-like droppings on attacked materials. Where you find those signs is where to concentrate drying, cleaning and treatment, and our guide to <a href="/small-brown-bugs-in-house/">small brown bugs in the house</a> helps if you are finding other insects in the same places.
          </p>

          <p>
            Valuables deserve special care. Old photographs, documents, books and wallpaper with starch-based paste are all at risk, and the damage is permanent. If you have family papers in a damp basement, moving them upstairs into a dry closet in sealed containers is the right first step regardless of how bad the silverfish problem is, because the humidity alone is slowly damaging them too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treatments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Treatments That Work Alongside the Dehumidifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticky traps come first, not because they control a large population but because they tell you where it is. NC State Extension and UC IPM both recommend them for locating and monitoring infestations, and NC State notes they may control small populations on their own. Place cockroach-style glue traps flat along walls, in corners, under sinks and beside stored items, check them after a week, and note which locations catch the most. That map tells you where to focus everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diatomaceous earth is a fine powder of fossilised algae that damages the waxy outer layer of insects and causes them to dry out, which makes it a natural partner for a dehumidifier. UC IPM notes it stays effective indefinitely in dry locations but cakes and loses effect when it gets damp, so dry the space first and apply it second. Use a thin, barely visible dusting in cracks, behind baseboards and along wall edges, not heaps on the floor that insects simply walk around, and wear a dust mask while applying it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Boric acid is the other long-standing option. It is sold as dusts for cracks and voids and as baits, and NC State Extension notes that boric acid baits are used in museums to protect collections and can work in homes when used properly. Apply dusts into wall voids, behind outlet covers with the power off, and in cracks where children and pets cannot reach, and follow the label exactly. As noted above, UC IPM considers boric acid dust alone ineffective against firebrats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing completes the picture. Caulk gaps along baseboards, around pipe penetrations under sinks and behind toilets, and along cracks in walls and around window and door trim. NC State Extension recommends sealing cracks and crevices that offer shelter or entry points. Fix leaking pipes and dripping taps, make sure bathroom exhaust fans work and are used, and insulate cold water pipes that sweat in summer. Each of these removes a damp refuge the dehumidifier would otherwise have to fight.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Role</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes from extension guidance</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dehumidifier at 45-50%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long-term prevention</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recommended by UC IPM and NC State for damp enclosed spaces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sticky traps</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Locate and monitor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">May control small populations alone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Diatomaceous earth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduce population</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lasts in dry spots; cakes when damp</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Boric acid dust or bait</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduce population</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Use in voids and cracks; dust alone weak on firebrats</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealing and leak repair</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove refuges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Closes the damp gaps a dehumidifier cannot reach</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For severe infestations, extension services also mention residual insecticide sprays and dusts labelled for silverfish, applied to cracks and voids rather than broadcast. NC State notes that fogging attics rarely works because the chemical does not reach into insulation and stored items. If you choose to use any insecticide, read and follow the label, keep children and pets away from treated areas, and consider whether a professional treatment would be safer for your situation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Four-Week Plan, and When to Call a Professional
          </h2>

          <p>
            Week one is diagnosis and drying. Put out sticky traps in at least six to eight locations and place a hygrometer in the dampest-feeling spot. Start the dehumidifier at 45 to 50 percent with continuous drainage if you can arrange it, and look for the water source: leaks, sweating pipes, a bathroom fan that is not running, a damp wall. Fix what you can immediately and make a list of what needs a contractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Week two is food and clutter. Recycle cardboard and old paper, move what you are keeping into lidded plastic bins on shelves, store dry food and pet food in sealed containers, and vacuum thoroughly along every baseboard. Check the traps from week one and move a few to the spots that caught the most. By now the hygrometer should be holding near your target; if it is not, the dehumidifier is undersized or the space has more water coming in than you thought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Week three is treatment and sealing. With the space dry, apply a thin dusting of diatomaceous earth or a boric acid dust into the cracks and voids near your hot spots, or place labelled baits there, and caulk the gaps along baseboards and pipe penetrations. Week four is review. Count what the traps are catching compared with week one. A clear fall means the plan is working, and the dehumidifier now keeps it that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Call a professional if trap counts are not falling after two to three months of consistent drying and cleaning, if you are finding damage on valuable books, documents or textiles, or if you live in an apartment or attached home where the population may be coming from next door. A licensed pest control operator can identify the species, find voids you cannot reach and apply products that are not sold to homeowners. It is not usually needed, but it is not a failure to ask.
          </p>

          <p>
            And keep the dehumidifier. The most common way silverfish return is a household that dries the basement for one summer, sees the problem disappear and switches the machine off. Humidity climbs back above 60 percent the following year, the few survivors in the walls find conditions good again, and the cycle restarts. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier picks</a> focus on units that drain continuously and hold their target reliably, which is what long-term silverfish prevention actually needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Dehumidifier to Work in Days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adult silverfish can survive months without food and shelter in pockets that dry slowly. A dehumidifier alone shows its effect over a season, not a week. Pair it with food removal, sealing and a dust or bait if you want sightings to fall within weeks, and do not give up on drying because the first fortnight looks unchanged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drying the Room but Not the Hiding Spots</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A room can read 50 percent at the dehumidifier while the inside of a cardboard box against a cold wall is far wetter. Get boxes off the floor and away from exterior walls, replace cardboard with plastic bins, and put a hygrometer where the insects actually are, not beside the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Piling Diatomaceous Earth in a Damp Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diatomaceous earth works by drying insects out and loses its effect when it absorbs moisture, so spreading it in a damp basement wastes it. Dry the space first. Then use a thin, barely visible layer in cracks and along edges; insects avoid thick piles rather than walking through them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Firebrats Like Silverfish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mottled insects clustering near the furnace, water heater or dryer are probably firebrats, which tolerate dry, hot conditions that would drive silverfish out. A dehumidifier does little for them, and boric acid dust alone is weak against them. Seal around the appliance, clear stored paper and use a labelled product instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching the Machine Off Once They Are Gone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Silverfish problems most often return the summer after a household stops dehumidifying. A few survivors in wall voids are enough to restart the population once humidity climbs back above 60 percent. Keep the dehumidifier running at 45 to 50 percent through every humid season, ideally on continuous drainage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dehumidifier get rid of silverfish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It makes the house hostile to them, which is the most important long-term step, but it rarely clears an existing infestation alone. Silverfish need high humidity, and holding 45 to 50 percent removes that. Combine it with removing paper and cardboard, sealing cracks and using diatomaceous earth, boric acid or traps to reduce the population already present.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity do silverfish need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Extension sources put their preferred range high. Texas A&M AgriLife cites 75 to 95 percent relative humidity at 70 to 80 degrees, and UC IPM says they need above 75 percent at warmer temperatures. Keeping a basement at 45 to 50 percent is well below that and is also in the range generally recommended for limiting mould and dust mites.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take a dehumidifier to get rid of silverfish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its own, expect a gradual decline over a season, because adults can live months without food and survive in damp pockets. With food removal, sealing and a dust or bait added, most households see sightings drop within a few weeks, continuing over two to three months as eggs that were already laid hatch into dry conditions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do silverfish mean I have a moisture problem?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, yes. Regular silverfish sightings point to somewhere in the home staying damp: a bathroom without a working fan, a leak under a sink, sweating pipes, a damp basement wall or a roof leak. Finding and fixing that source does more than any treatment, and a hygrometer will show you which rooms run above 60 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are silverfish harmful?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Extension sources treat them as a nuisance and a pest of paper, books and fabric rather than a health threat; they are not known to bite people. The damage they do is to stored materials: holes in paper, scraped photographs, damaged book bindings and starched fabrics. Move valuable papers and photos into sealed containers somewhere dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for silverfish?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Size it for the space, not the insects. A damp 1,000-square-foot basement typically needs a 50-pint unit in current ratings, and smaller damp rooms a 22 to 35 pint unit. Continuous drainage matters more than usual, because a unit that stops on a full bucket lets humidity climb back up between emptyings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier in the basement stop silverfish upstairs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only partly. A basement unit dries the basement and helps the floor above a little, but a damp upstairs bathroom or a leak under the kitchen sink creates its own habitat. Treat each damp area: run bathroom exhaust fans, fix leaks and, where a room stays above 60 percent, consider a small unit for that room.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

