        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A dehumidifier does get rid of silverfish in the sense that matters most: it takes away the humid air they need, so the population cannot sustain itself and does not come back. It does not do it quickly, and it does not do it alone.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Hold the space at 45 to 50 percent, replace cardboard with sealed bins, seal the cracks and voids, and use traps, diatomaceous earth or boric acid to bring the existing numbers down. Then keep the machine running every humid season. If the insects are mottled and clustered around the furnace, they are firebrats, and the plan shifts from drying to sealing and treating.
          </p>
        </div>

